===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|lob|t=a lazy lout, bundle of clothing}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*lobb}}, {{m|ang|*lobbe}} word for lumpish or unwieldy things, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*lubbǭ||that which hangs or dangles}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*lewbʰ-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*lep-||to peel, skin}}.  Compare {{cog|da|lobbes||bumpkin, clown}}, {{cog|ang|loppe||spider}} (in the sense of something that hangs or dangles).  Possibly influenced or borrowed through {{der|en|cy|llob||lump}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[lump]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|author=M.L. Kenny|title=The fortunes of Maurice Cronin| page=126| url=http://books.google.com/books?id=x_0BAAAAQAAJ| passage=And, moreover, I had no sooner set my eyes on the keys, than I remembered where there was a '''lob''' of money lying in Purcell's safe, that I &mdash;}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[country bumpkin]]; a [[yokel]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:country bumpkin}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|act=II|scene=i|passage=Farewell, thou '''lob''' of spirits; I'll be gone: Our queen and all her elves come here anon.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1694|author=w:Peter Anthony Motteux|title= s:The Fourth Book| by=w:Rabelais| section=[[wikisource:The Fourth Book/Chapter XLVII| Chapter XLVII]]| passage=THE country '''lob''' trudged home very much concerned and thoughtful, you may swear; insomuch that his good woman, seeing him thus look moping, weened that something had been stolen from him at market {{...}}}}
# A [[clumsy]] person.
# The person who comes last in a [[race]].
# A [[lob-worm]].

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|lump}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|lob|t=pollock}}, cognate with {{cog|dum,gml|lobbe|t=a type of small plump or stocky fish, cod}}, {{cog|da|lubbe}}, from {{der|en|non|lubba}}, ultimately from {{m|en|lob#Etymology_2|sense 2}} in the sense of "clumsy, heavily or lumpily hanging."

